[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Embedded Objects and Element Collections in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java Persistence API (JPA), modeling complex domain models often requires encapsulating reusable state within an entity or storing collections of basic types and embedded objects. JPA provides the @Embeddable, @Embedded, and @ElementCollection annotations to handle these scenarios efficiently without creating unnecessary entity relationships."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding @Embeddable and @Embedded"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An embeddable object is a class that holds state but does not have its own persistent identity (primary key). It is stored as part of the owning entity's table in the database. Use the @Embeddable annotation on the class definition and the @Embedded annotation within the target entity."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Embeddable
public class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;
    private String zipCode;

    // Getters and setters
}"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @Embedded
    private Address address;

    // Getters and setters
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Overriding Embeddable Attributes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"If an entity uses the same embeddable class multiple times, or if you need to customize column names specifically for a particular entity mapping, you can use the @AttributeOverrides and @AttributeOverride annotations."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "employees")
public class Employee {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @Embedded
    @AttributeOverrides({
        @AttributeOverride(name = "street", column = @Column(name = "home_street")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "city", column = @Column(name = "home_city")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "zipCode", column = @Column(name = "home_zip"))
    })
    private Address homeAddress;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with @ElementCollection"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @ElementCollection annotation is used to map a collection of basic types (like String, Integer) or a collection of @Embeddable objects. Unlike @OneToMany, an element collection does not represent a relationship to another entity. The lifecycle of the collection elements is strictly tied to the owning entity."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class UserProfile {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "user_nicknames", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "user_id"))
    @Column(name = "nickname")
    private Set<String> nicknames = new HashSet<>();

    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "user_addresses", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "user_id"))
    private List<Address> pastAddresses = new ArrayList<>();
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Considerations"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always implement equals() and hashCode() using business keys for objects inside an @ElementCollection to prevent duplicate entries in Sets."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer List or Set over arrays for collections to maintain proper JPA state management."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use @CollectionTable to explicitly define the table and foreign key names for element collections, avoiding default naming conventions that might conflict."}]
